{"id": "x0521", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 34, "onset_chunk": 33, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "On Friday morning, the neighbors put out 18 pink fl amingos. Then on Saturday, they took back one-third of those 18, which is 6, and painted them white. So that leaves 12 pink ones still in the yard. On Sunday, they added another 18 pink flaming os. That brings the total pink flamingos to 12 plus 1 8, which is 30. The white ones are still just the 6 they repainted. So at noon on Sunday, there are 30 pink flamingos and 6 white ones. That means there are 24 more pink fl amingos than white ones.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 22, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0529", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 25, "onset_chunk": 24, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, let's break this down. First, the New York Times: six copies at $ 5 each is $ 30. His 10% commission on that is $ 3. Now for the Wall Street Journal: ten copies at $15 each is $ 15 0. His 8% commission there is $12. Add ing them together, $3 plus $12 gives him a total commission of $1 5.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 22, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0537", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 19, "onset_chunk": 17, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so let's figure this out. First, we need to find out how many trees the students plant. There are 4 0 classes with 25 students in each, so that ’s 4 0 times 25, which gives us 1 0 00 students total. Each student plants one tree, so they plant 1 000 trees. Next, for the teachers: there are 4 0 classes and 3 teachers per class, so that ’s 4 0 times 3, equal ing 12 0 teachers in total. Each teacher plants two trees, so 12 0 times 2 is 2 40 trees. Now, add the student trees and the teacher trees together: 1 0 00 plus 2 4 0 equal s 1 2 4 0 trees. So, at the end of the campaign, Ashley’s school will have planted 1 2 40 trees in total.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0545", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 15, "onset_chunk": 14, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "If a gallon jar costs five dollars and a half gal lon jar cost s two dollars less, then it costs five minus two which is three dollars. To buy ten gal lon jars, C ecilia spent 10 times five dollars which is fifty dollars. To buy sixteen half gallon jars, she spent 16 times three dollars which is forty-eight dollars. The total amount of money she spent on all the jars is fifty plus forty- eight which equals ninety- eight dollars.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 30, "carrier_ended": null}
